

I have worked on a hybrid automation framework using Selenium with Java,
Cucumber for BDD, and TestNG and Appium and jenkins.

----we have a config folder with separate properties files for QA, staging, and production,
 each file holding the app URL, environment name, username, Password and browser.
  A ConfigReader class reads the right file based on a system property passed at runtime.

-----We have base Driver package contains DriverFactory, which handles driver creation based
    on the browser parameters and DriverManager, which holds the actual driver instance using a ThreadLocal.
    provides getter nad setter methods - the driver be shared safly between hook and step definations.
    Hooks sets the driver into DriverManager before a scenario starts,
    and my step definitions pull it back out from the same place.
    Using ThreadLocal specifically also means parallel scenario execution is safe.

----we follow Page Object Model. Each pages is its own class with three parts:
    locators declared with @FindBy, a constructor that takes the driver and
    calls PageFactory.initElements() to wire those locators up lazily,
    and action methods.

----We have Listeners package - we build two things @1- RetryAnalyzer implementing TestNG's IRetryAnalyzer-
    it retries a failed scenario immediately, in the same run, Given retry count.
    overrides the retry() method, which accepts an ITestResult object as a parameter.
    RetryTransformer implements IAnnotationTransformer and overrides its transform() method,
   which accepts an ITestAnnotation object — inside it, we call annotation.setRetryAnalyzer(RetryAnalyzer.class),
   which applies the retry logic to every test method automatically,
   Second, if a scenario is still failing after those retries, I capture evidence of it.
   In my Hooks class's @After method, I check scenario.isFailed()
   — if true, I use Cucumber's scenario.attach() to embed a screenshot directly into the report,
   and separately save a copy to disk too.

---For reporting itself, I used the Extent Reports Cucumber adapter,
   which hooks directly into Cucumber's plugin system and generates a report automatically.
   And separately from the in-run retry, Cucumber's own rerun plugin writes any scenario that's
   still failing to a file after the run finishes.
   I have a second runner, RerunFailedTests, that reads just that file and re-executes only those scenarios —
   typically run as its own stage in CI/CD, so a genuinely broken scenario gets one final,
    clean confirmation before it's reported as a real bug."

--- pom.xml has all my dependencies
 and configures the Surefire plugin two ways: it points Maven at testng.xml to actually drive execution,
 testng.xml itself just points to my TestRunner class and registers the retry listener,
 and also sets parallel execution at the suite level.
 And I have a Jenkinsfile that wraps all of this into a CI/CD pipeline,
 with environment and browser as pipeline parameters.